---
/**
 * Legacy URL: /custom-concrete/ (WP page ID 78).
 *
 * CONSOLIDATED 2026-09-19. This page absorbed /custom-concrete-driveways/
 * (WP page ID 192), which now 301s here — see public/_redirects and
 * src/pages/custom-concrete-driveways.astro.
 *
 * Why the merge, and why this URL: driveways sit under custom stamped concrete
 * rather than beside it, and two pages competing for the same stamped-concrete
 * terms split the signal between them. The driveway keyword does not live in
 * the slug any more, so it has to carry in the H1, the section H2s and the
 * anchors instead — which is what the PhotoSection ids are for. Do not rename
 * #driveways; it is linked from /services/ and is a public fragment.
 *
 * This page does NOT use ServiceLayout. The other service pages are one prose
 * block and one photo grid; this one is four topics that each need their own
 * heading and their own photographs, which is a different shape.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import PhotoSection from '../components/PhotoSection.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import { A } from '../lib/assets';
import { withBase } from '../lib/urls';
import { SITE_URL } from '../lib/site';

const driveways = [
  { asset: A.stampedConcreteDrivewayWithStoneRetainingWall,
    caption: 'Stamped driveway tied into a stone-faced retaining wall on the same job.' },
  { asset: A.paverDrivewayWithCustomConcretePillars,
    caption: 'Paver driveway framed by custom concrete pillars at the entry.' },
  { asset: A.spanishDrive,
    caption: 'Poured driveway and flagstone walkway to a Spanish-style frontage.' },
  { asset: A.fountainPavers,
    caption: 'Paver motor court with a custom concrete water feature set into it.' },
  { asset: A.concreteDrivewayWithStoneRetainingWalls,
    caption: 'Driveway held by stone retaining walls where the grade falls away.' },
  { asset: A.dosVientosDrive,
    caption: 'Stamped driveway running beside a stone-faced retaining wall, Dos Vientos.' },
  { asset: A.stoneRetainingWallAndStampedConcreteDriveway,
    caption: 'Stamped driveway and stone retaining wall poured as one job, Newbury Park.' },
  { asset: A.sidewalkFeaturesWithStampedConcreteAndStone,
    caption: 'Sidewalk banding and borders poured as part of the driveway, not added afterwards.' },
  { asset: A.cpfPaverDriveway,
    caption: 'Paver driveway laid on a compacted base, borders cut to the curve.' },
  { asset: A.stampedConcreteDrivewayAndRetainingWall,
    caption: 'Driveway and retaining wall set out and poured as one sequence.' },
  { asset: A.curvedDrive,
    caption: 'Curved paver driveway sweeping in front of a residence.' },
  { asset: A.paverDrive,
    caption: 'Paver driveway with planted borders in front of a home.' },
];

const flatwork = [
  { asset: A.herringboneClose,
    caption: 'Red brick paving laid in a herringbone pattern, photographed close.' },
  { asset: A.roseWalkway,
    caption: 'Paver walkway running between beds of flowering roses to a side gate.' },
  { asset: A.herringboneWet,
    caption: 'Herringbone paving with a damp finish, showing the clay color variation.' },
  { asset: A.curvedRoseWalk,
    caption: 'Curving paver walkway alongside rose beds leading to a side entry.' },
  { asset: A.brickEntryWalkwayCurvedPlanterWalls,
    caption: 'Brick entry walkway laid in herringbone between curved brick planter walls.' },
  { asset: A.ledLitCurvedConcreteWalkway,
    caption: 'Curved concrete walkway with lighting run under the edge, washing the path and the beds alongside it.' },
  { asset: A.potteryEntry,
    caption: 'Home entry with potted plants set over paver flooring.' },
  { asset: A.fencedWalkway,
    caption: 'Paver walkway with a black metal fence and a clipped hedge.' },
  { asset: A.driveApron,
    caption: 'Paved driveway apron and walkway at a residential garage.' },
  { asset: A.stampedConcreteWalkwayProgressShot,
    caption: 'Stamped walkway mid-pour — the stamp goes in during a window measured in minutes.' },
  { asset: A.stampedConcreteProgressShot,
    caption: 'Flatwork being finished before the stamp, with the joints already set out.' },
];

const commercial = [
  { asset: A.customCommercialSignAtOaksMall,
    caption: 'Cast concrete monument sign at The Oaks, Thousand Oaks.' },
  { asset: A.oaksMonument,
    caption: 'The same monument from the approach, set into its planted surround.' },
  { asset: A.concreteCommercialSignAtMonteVistaChurch,
    caption: 'Commercial concrete signage for Monte Vista, Newbury Park.' },
  { asset: A.newburyParkMonteVistaConcreteSign,
    caption: 'The Monte Vista sign in its finished landscaping.' },
];

const description =
  'Custom and stamped concrete in Newbury Park, Thousand Oaks and the Conejo Valley: stamped and colored concrete driveways, paver driveways, patios, walkways and commercial concrete. Family owned, owner supervised, licensed and insured. CA License #878989, C-29 / C-8.';
---

<Layout
  title="Custom & Stamped Concrete"
  description={description}
  extraSchema={[
    {
      '@type': 'Service',
      name: 'Custom and stamped concrete, including concrete driveways',
      serviceType: 'Custom and stamped concrete',
      description,
      provider: { '@id': `${SITE_URL}/#business` },
      areaServed: ['Newbury Park', 'Thousand Oaks', 'Westlake Village', 'Calabasas', 'Camarillo'].map(
        (n) => ({ '@type': 'City', name: n }),
      ),
    },
  ]}
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Concrete"
    title="Custom & Stamped Concrete"
    intro="Stamped and colored concrete driveways, paver driveways, patios, walkways and commercial flatwork — poured across Newbury Park, Thousand Oaks and the wider Conejo Valley."
    asset={A.stampedDriveway}
  />

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 prose-cpf">
    <h2>Stamped concrete in Newbury Park and Thousand Oaks</h2>
    <p>
      Concrete is the largest single surface most homes have, and it is the one
      people look at every day on the way to the front door. A driveway that
      cracks along the wrong line, or a patio that holds water in the middle, is
      expensive to put right — so the value sits in the sub-base, the
      reinforcement and the joint layout long before anyone sees a finish.
    </p>
    <p>
      Stamping takes a poured slab and gives it the pattern and texture of
      stone, brick or tile at a fraction of the cost of laying the real material
      over that area. The result is only as good as the timing: stamps go in
      during a window measured in minutes, and a crew that misses it leaves the
      evidence in the surface permanently. Colors are chosen against the house —
      the roof tile, the stone, the render — rather than off a chart, because a
      stamp that fights the elevation reads as obviously applied.
    </p>

    <h3>What that covers</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong><a href="#driveways">Driveways and aprons</a></strong> — formed, reinforced and poured, with jointing set out to control where it cracks.</li>
      <li><strong>Stamped and colored flatwork</strong> — patterns and release colors matched to the house.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="#flatwork">Patios and paver walkways</a></strong> — laid on a proper base so they stay flat.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="#commercial">Commercial concrete</a></strong> — monument signage and site flatwork.</li>
      <li><strong><a href={withBase('/concrete-countertops/')}>Poured countertops</a></strong> — indoor and outdoor, on their own page.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <PhotoSection
    id="driveways"
    heading="Concrete driveways"
    lede="A driveway is the largest single piece of concrete on most properties and the first thing anyone sees. It is also the piece that takes the most abuse: vehicle loads, a full temperature swing every day, and whatever the ground underneath decides to do over twenty years. A driveway that cracks in its third summer was almost never a finishing problem — it was the base, the thickness, or the control joints."
    items={driveways}
    tone="surface"
  />

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 prose-cpf">
    <h3>Paver driveways</h3>
    <p>
      Pavers buy you something concrete cannot: individual units that can be
      lifted and re-laid. If a utility has to come up, or a section settles, the
      repair is invisible. The tradeoff is the base preparation, which is more
      exacting than for a slab, not less.
    </p>

    <h3>Everything the driveway touches</h3>
    <p>
      Most of these jobs are not only a driveway. The grade has to be resolved
      where it meets the street, the garage slab and the front walk, and on a
      sloping lot that usually means a retaining wall in the same pour sequence.
      Doing it as one job is why the banding, the borders and the wall all line
      up — see the <a href={withBase('/stonework/')}>stonework</a> and
      <a href={withBase('/grading-and-excavation/')}>grading and excavation</a>
      pages for the parts of that work that happen before any concrete arrives.
    </p>

    <h3>Working on a slope</h3>
    <p>
      Much of the Conejo Valley is built on grade. Flatwork on a slope is a
      drainage problem before it is a concrete problem: where the water goes at
      the bottom of a driveway decides whether the slab lasts. Falls, drains and
      the transition into the retaining structure get designed together, not
      bolted on afterwards.
    </p>
  </article>

  <PhotoSection
    id="flatwork"
    heading="Patios, walkways and paver flatwork"
    lede="The same base work decides whether a patio or a walkway stays flat. These are laid on a compacted base with the falls set before anything is placed, so the surface drains where it is supposed to."
    items={flatwork}
    tone="surface"
  />

  <PhotoSection
    id="commercial"
    heading="Commercial concrete and monument signage"
    lede="Cast concrete signage and site flatwork for commercial clients across Ventura County, including The Oaks in Thousand Oaks and Monte Vista in Newbury Park."
    items={commercial}
  />

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 prose-cpf">
    <h2>Owner supervised, fully insured</h2>
    <p>
      A concrete pour takes the driveway out of use, and an open form is not
      something anyone wants sitting for a fortnight. <strong>Each project is
      owner supervised</strong> — we are the first in and the last out — and CPF
      is <strong>licensed, insured and bonded</strong> under C-29 masonry and
      C-8 concrete, which is the part homeowners actually worry about.
    </p>
  </article>

  <CtaBand />
</Layout>
